Title: Bluetooth tester application

Description:

Tester application uses binary protocol to control Zephyr stack and is aimed at
automated testing. It requires two serial ports to operate.
The first serial is used by Bluetooth Testing Protocol (BTP) to drive Bluetooth
stack. BTP commands and events are received and buffered for further processing
over the same serial.

BTP specification can be found in auto-pts project repository:
https://github.com/intel/auto-pts
The auto-pts is an automation framework for PTS Bluetooth testing tool provided
by Bluetooth SIG.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Supported Profiles:

MMDL

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

MMDL Retained mode:

The Tester keeps the Node provisioned between the MMDL Test Cases. When the MMDL
BTP Service is registered, the next MESH_START clears the test state of the
previous Test Case. This reduces the MMDL Test Suite run time significantly:
during tests, captured a reduced completion of the MMDL Test Suite by about 45%.

On `MESH_START`, before settings are loaded, the Tester deletes the test state of
the previous Test Case from the settings: Model states, publications,
subscriptions, label UUIDs, Configuration Server state and Heartbeat
publication. The network data, NetKeys, AppKeys, IV Index, SEQ number, RPL and
AppKey bindings are kept. The Tester also keeps the address of the Node that
bound the AppKeys.

The test state is cleared only when the MMDL BTP Service is registered, which
AutoPTS does in the Test Case pre-conditions. A reset of the IUT inside a Test
Case does not register the MMDL BTP Service again, so the states stored before
such a reset are kept, as the Test Case expects.

If the Node is already provisioned on `MESH_START`, the Tester does not open
the PB-ADV again. It restores the network context and sends `MESH_EV_PROVISIONED`
to AutoPTS Client.

Client Models send messages to the publication address configured by the
current Test Case. If there is no publication, they send messages to PTS, with
the lowest AppKey Index bound to the Model, on the subnet of that AppKey.

Mode Requirements:

- The IUT must be reset (without erasing flash) before each Test Case.
  The provisioning data stays in the settings, and only the test state is
  cleared on `MESH_START`.
  If the Node is already running when `MESH_START` is received, the command
  fails and the Tester logs "Test state clear requested on a running node: IUT
  was not reset before the Test Case.".
- AutoPTS must include the MMDL retained mode support. It sends
  `MESH_CONFIG_PROVISIONING` and `MESH_START` in the Test Case pre-conditions
  and sets the `TSPX_delete_link_key` and `TSPX_delete_ltk` PIXITs to FALSE.
  Retained mode is enabled by default. To provision the IUT in every Test Case
  instead, set the environment variable `AUTOPTS_MMDL_SECURITY_INFORMATION=0`
  before running AutoPTS.
- With RTT logging enabled, run AutoPTS with `--pylink_reset`. nrfutil may fail
  to reset the IUT while the RTT logger holds the J-Link connection, and the
  IUT is then not reset when PTS requests it.

IUT Recovery:

If PTS has no security information but the IUT is still provisioned, PTS asks
to remove the security information (WID 13). AutoPTS then sends `MESH_RESET`
automatically, and PTS provisions the IUT again. No manual action is needed.

If PTS and the IUT both hold security information but the keys do not match (for
example, the IUT has been provisioned with another PTS workspace), PTS does not
ask for WID 13 and the Test Cases time out. In this case, erase the IUT flash
or send `MESH_RESET` to the IUT before the next run.

After programming firmware with a different Composition Data content (e.g.
different set of Elements and/or Models), erase the IUT flash. The AppKey
bindings are stored per Model index, so the stored bindings would not match the
new Composition Data.
After the flash is erased, PTS provisions and configures the IUT again in the
first Test Case.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Building and running on QEMU:

QEMU should have connection with the external host Bluetooth hardware.
The btproxy tool from BlueZ can be used to give access to a Bluetooth controller
attached to the Linux host OS:

$ sudo tools/btproxy -u
Listening on /tmp/bt-server-bredr

/tmp/bt-server-bredr option is already set in Makefile through QEMU_EXTRA_FLAGS.

To build tester application for QEMU use BOARD=qemu_cortex_m3 and
CONF_FILE=qemu.conf. After this qemu can be started through the "run"
build target.

Note: Target board have to support enough UARTs for BTP and controller.
      We recommend using qemu_cortex_m3.

'bt-stack-tester' UNIX socket (previously set in Makefile) can be used for now
to control tester application.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Next, build and flash tester application by employing the "flash" build
target.

Use serial client, e.g. PUTTY to communicate over the serial port
(typically /dev/ttyUSBx) with the tester using BTP.

